.Dd August 17, 2026
.Dt SOWA-BOOTTIME 8
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm sowa-boottime
.Nd what the boot and the shutdown spent their time on
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Nm
.Fl a | -all
.Nm
.Fl b | -boot Ar id
.Nm
.Fl H | -history
.Nm
.Fl s | -slowest Op Ar n
.Nm
.Fl f | -file Ar path
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints what each init script cost, in the order the scripts ran.
With no options it shows the boot that is running.
.Pp
The numbers are not estimated.
Every Sowa init script announces what it is about to do with
.Fn begin
and reports how it went with
.Fn ok ,
.Fn failed
or
.Fn skipped ,
and those helpers in
.Pa /etc/rc.d/init.d/functions
time the gap between the two and file a record.
.Pa /etc/rc.d/rc ,
the runlevel driver, files one for a whole pass, and
.Pa /etc/rc.d/rc.sysinit
files one for the mounting that happens before any runlevel, which is what makes
the gap between the kernel handing over and the first service starting visible
rather than inferred.
.Pp
The clock is
.Pa /proc/uptime ,
which is monotonic.
A wall clock would do here for nothing:
.Xr chronyd 8
starts in the middle of the boot being measured and may step the clock by years,
and a service that straddled the step would come out having taken less than no
time.
.Pp
Two decimal places is all
.Pa /proc/uptime
offers, so that is the resolution of every number here.
.Ss The files
.Bl -tag -width "/var/log/service-timing.log"
.It Pa /run/sowa/timing
The boot that is running.
.Pa /run
is the tmpfs
.Pa /etc/rc.d/rc.sysinit
mounts, so this file cannot outlive the boot it describes - which is the point:
it is never a record of some earlier boot mistaken for this one.
.It Pa /var/log/service-timing.log
Every boot that has left a record on this machine.
This is the one with the shutdowns in it, because a shutdown's records are
written seconds before the machine stops existing and
.Pa /run
goes with it.
.El
.Pp
Each line of both is:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<boot> <at> <took> <outcome> <service> <label...>
.Ed
.Pp
.Ar boot
is the first field of the kernel's per-boot UUID from
.Pa /proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id ,
which is what tells one boot's records from the next.
.Ar at
is when the action started, in seconds since the kernel did, and
.Ar took
is how long it ran.
.Ar outcome
is
.Cm ok ,
.Cm failed
or
.Cm skip .
The label runs to the end of the line, so it is last.
.Pp
Nothing rotates the history.
It grows by roughly a hundred bytes per boot, which is the bargain
.Pa /var/log/wtmp
is already on in this image; delete it and it starts again.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-s, --slowest n"
.It Fl a , -all
Every boot in the history, oldest first, each as its own timeline.
.It Fl b , -boot Ar id
One boot, named by the id printed at the head of a timeline.
This reads the history rather than
.Pa /run ,
since a boot that is not this one is only recorded there.
.It Fl H , -history
One line per boot: how many actions it ran, what its first runlevel pass cost
coming up, what its last one cost going down, and how many actions failed.
This is the view for
.Dq the boot got slower :
two numbers per boot, comparable down the column.
A boot still running has no shutdown figure yet and shows
.Ql -
in its place.
.It Fl s , -slowest Op Ar n
The
.Ar n
slowest actions on record, whichever boot each belongs to.
The default is 10.
What to look at first when a boot has become slower and it is not obvious which
part of it did.
.It Fl f , -file Ar path
Read this file rather than either of the usual two.
.It Fl h , -help
Print the usage and exit.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/rc.d/init.d/functions"
.It Pa /run/sowa/timing
The boot that is running.
.It Pa /var/log/service-timing.log
Every boot on record.
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/init.d/functions
The helpers that write both.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 2n
.It 0
The records were read.
.It 1
There are none to read, and why is on standard error.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
What this boot did, and in what order:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# sowa-boottime
Boot 222e8b0a, kernel 6.18.42, up 12s

        at      took  how     service      what
      1.01      0.20  ok      rc.sysinit   Mounting and setting up
      1.21      0.23  ok      zram         Starting zram
      1.45      3.21  ok      network      Configuring the network
      4.67      0.24  ok      chronyd      Starting chronyd
      4.91      0.42  ok      sshd         Starting sshd
      5.33      0.15  ok      crond        Starting crond

  6 actions, 4.45s of them spent working, last one done at 5.48s
  Runlevel 3 took 4.27s, starting at 1.21s
.Ed
.Pp
The network is what that boot was waiting for, and the figure is nearly all
.Ic nic Ns 's
wait for IPv6 duplicate address detection rather than the DHCP lease.
See the comment at the top of
.Pa /etc/rc.d/init.d/network .
.Pp
Whether it has been getting worse:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# sowa-boottime --history
.Ed
.Pp
And what to blame if it has:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# sowa-boottime --slowest 5
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr inittab 5 ,
.Xr chkconfig 8 ,
.Xr init 8 ,
.Xr service 8
.Sh CAVEATS
An action is timed between the
.Fn begin
that announced it and the marker that ended it, so what is measured is what the
init script did - not what the daemon it started went on to do afterwards.
.Xr chronyd 8
is recorded as costing a quarter of a second because that is what starting it
costs; stepping the clock to match a server happens later and is not here.
.Pp
A service whose script neither prints a marker nor calls
.Fn result
leaves no record.
Nothing in the base image is such a script.
